ight vertex, so","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"no self-loop. Simple: an edge is inserted once, in either orientation. Each edge is stored left","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"endpoint first, so ","type":"text"},{"code":"edges[e].u","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is its left end.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/bipartitegraphs\/bipartitegraph","identifier":"doc:\/\/BipartiteGraphs\/documentation\/BipartiteGraphs\/BipartiteGraph","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"BipartiteGraph"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"BipartiteGraph"}],"type":"topic","kind":"symbol"},"doc://BipartiteGraphs/documentation/BipartiteGraphs/BipartiteGraph/findOddCycle()":{"title":"findOddCycle()","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"An odd cycle, or nil when the graph is bipartite (Boost "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"find_odd_cycle"},{"type":"text","text":"): the first one a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"breadth-first two-colouring meets (roots in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" order, rows in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"order), closed through the conflicting edge’s ends’ lowest common ancestor in the search"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"tree. A self-loop is an odd cycle of length 1. In Cycles’ canonical form: it starts at its"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"least vertex and leaves through the lesser of its two edges. Not necessarily a shortest odd"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"cycle. O(n + m)."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/BipartiteGraphs\/documentation\/BipartiteGraphs\/BipartiteGraph\/findOddCycle()","url":"\/documentation\/bipartitegraphs\/bipartitegraph\/findoddcycle()","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text